  data:  # 13fc
    kind: work_item  # 13fc
    title: "Core scope-aware name resolution: give the shared resolvers (NameResolver/ListNameResolver) a lexical scope and call-shape filter instead of fixing ~29 per-analyzer short-name defects one by one"  # 13fc
    status: todo_hard  # 13fc
    priority: 2  # 13fc
    parent:  # 13fc
    tags:  # 13fc
    - resolution  # 13fc
    - call_graph  # 13fc
    - analysis_quality  # 13fc
    isbefore: []  # 13fc
    duplicate_of: []  # 13fc
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 13fc
    pr_ref:  # 13fc
    description: "PRE-APPROVED BY THE OWNER (2026-11-01), END TO END. Every phase below, every design choice inside it, every PR (auto-pr, self-merge), any ADR edit or new ADR, and any re-scoping the evidence calls for are approved in advance. The owner's expectation: when the agent or a subagent is uncertain, it INVESTIGATES CAREFULLY — in light of docs/adr/, docs/hypergumbo-spec.md and the hypergumbo spirit (positive claims over inferred emptiness, a wrong edge is worse than a missing one, measure before sizing, ship the scope you measured, structural over per-instance) — and DECIDES, recording the reasoning on this item. Do not interrupt work to ask the owner a question the owner would almost certainly answer with \"investigate carefully and come back with a recommended path forward\": do that investigation yourself and proceed on your recommendation. Escalate only what investigation genuinely cannot settle (e.g. two owner rulings that collide), and then bring a recommendation, not an open question.\n\nPROBLEM. The call graph resolves callee names without a lexical scope or a call-shape model, so it emits WRONG edges that look right (a wrong edge reads as recall; a missing one at least shows as an unresolved placeholder). Every consumer inherits it: slice, reverse slice, tiers, centrality, dead code, taint reach. Verified in code on dev fbff2b8a04:\n- packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/symbol_resolution.py: ListNameResolver.lookup step 1 is `name in self.registry` (a repo-wide bare-name table); step 2 is suffix matching (\"doWork\" matches \"MyClass.doWork\"). Neither NameResolver.lookup nor ListNameResolver.lookup accepts a lexical scope or a call shape/kind; the only disambiguators are path hints, a test-path preference, and allow_suffix.\n- 77 of the 143 language-package modules import one of these resolvers — the chokepoint already exists.\n- The scope pieces exist but are unshared or unadopted: hypergumbo_core/import_scope.py ImportScope has ZERO adopters (its own docstring says no analyzer has ever constructed it); position-aware binders exist only per language (js_ts _ScopeBinder, python _pyscope.ScopeStack).\n\nFAMILIES THIS ADDRESSES (tracker hygiene sweep 2026-11-01, ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/tracker_hygiene_sweep_2026-11-01T223538Z/meta_analysis_integrated.md, theme D, 62 items in 17 families; members are SAME_MECHANISM per analyzer today):\n- F-name-lookup-unscoped (8): INV-hakof, WI-dagig, WI-lijab, WI-lijin, WI-rafin, WI-vipir, WI-vuzon, WI-zurum\n- F-local-type-scope (5): INV-hovoj, WI-fogoj, WI-kizih, WI-masov, WI-nazub\n- F-import-binding-layer (3): INV-damas, WI-bohih, WI-tular\n- F-kind-unfiltered-lookup (4): WI-dibiv, WI-surid, WI-zutof (WI-jalal folded into WI-surid)\n- F-qualifier-dropped (3): WI-kazut, WI-niriz, WI-zafin\n- F-last-write-wins-registry, in part (6): WI-dabul, WI-hahud, WI-hubik, WI-povub, WI-sipoj (WI-pagoj folded into WI-dabul)\nAbout 29 filed items; the same mechanism very likely has unfiled instances in analyzers nobody has swept. Neighbours that are NOT the same fix but should be read first: F-enclosing-symbol (the edge SOURCE; cure is core base.enclosing_declared_symbol), F-overload-underdetermined (core overload_arity.choose_overload), WI-javaf (no 'bound by no import' marker on unresolved bare-name calls). Prior symptom-level work to read, not redo: WI-malur (ambiguity guard in ListNameResolver), WI-kopuz (short-name registration removed in 10 languages), WI-himod / ADR-0039 ruling 6 (resolver match quality enters confidence only as resolution_score, interpolated in the pathway band — a new demotion must go through that, not a private constant). Also ADR-0007 (import tracking for call resolution) and ADR-0006 (return-type registry).\n\nPLAN (phase order is a real dependency; refine it if the evidence says so):\n1. MEASURE FIRST (read-only). Instrument which lookup step produced each resolved call edge (exact / path hint / suffix / ambiguous, and whether a scope-correct binding existed) on a curriculum corpus spanning the affected languages, plus self-analysis. Diff by call site and read random samples per class to estimate the wrong-edge rate per step and per language. Record the numbers on this item. If the wrong-edge rate is small, say so and re-scope — do not build phases 2-3 on an unmeasured premise.\n2. ONE SITE. Give the shared resolvers a caller-scope input and a call-shape/kind admit predicate. A short-name or suffix hit with no scope evidence becomes either a lower resolution_score (ADR-0039 ruling 6) or an unresolved placeholder rather than a confident edge — choose on the phase-1 evidence and the ADRs. This tightens an activation gate (recall-loss direction): pre-register the expected effect, then run a corpus A/B and report edges lost and gained by call site, including the correct edges lost.\n3. ONE CORE BINDER. Build a core position-aware scope binder generalising _ScopeBinder and ScopeStack, fed through ImportScope's add_* methods, and adopt it analyzer by analyzer (largest phase-1 wrong-edge share first). The resolution logic is written once; each analyzer only reports its bindings.\n\nACCEPTANCE. Phase-1 numbers recorded here. Each family member above is closed on its OWN filed repro (closure-evidence discipline), or re-scoped with the residual filed. Corpus A/B results on this item; awaits_bakeoff_validation tag on any PR claiming a quantitative improvement. No raised shrink-only baseline."  # 13fc
    fields: {}  # 13fc
- op: discuss  # a630
  at: '2026-11-02T01:54:04Z'  # a630
  by: agent  # a630
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a630
  clock: 2  # a630
  nonce: a630  # a630
  message: "WI-jomik PHASE 1 RESULT (2026-11-02, dev 64a87d13ff; lab ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/jomik_phase1_11012026/: harness.py, analyze.py, sample.py, estimate.py, shape.py, BRIEF.md, verdicts_*.jsonl, judged_final.json).\n\nINSTRUMENT. In-process harness (no code change) wraps NameResolver/ListNameResolver/SymbolResolver lookups and Edge construction, tagging each call edge with the lookup step that produced it (a lookup is consumed by the first edge to its symbol built inside the same or the helper-caller frame), then joins to the final survey by (src, dst, line). Scope evidence per edge, post hoc: same_file / imported_file (an imports edge between the two files) / same_dir / none; shared_name = another callable in the repo has the same short name. CONTROL fixture (3 JS files, known steps) passes; it caught a stale-attribution bug in v1 of the harness (fixed, rerun) and REPRODUCED A WRONG EDGE: b.js `shared()` with no import binds to the function NESTED inside c.js's outer(), not a.js's top-level shared (repo-wide bare-name table, last write wins, nested declarations registered as globals).\n\nCORPUS (11 repos, 66,414 resolved call edges): viper, wire (go); express, knex (js); jotai (ts); jedis (java); just (rust); httpx (python); tmux (c); git-crypt (cpp); hypergumbo-core src (python). NOT COVERED (no corpus repo): ruby, kotlin, scala, swift, php, ocaml, solidity, agda, starlark, llvm_ir, make -- the languages of ~12 of the family members.\n\n1. SHARE. 23,819 of 66,414 resolved call edges (36%) come from a shared-resolver lookup: c 7,749 (all c.py exact), js 5,081, rust 5,388, java 2,804, python 1,269, ts 891, go 473, cpp 156. The rest come from analyzer-private paths (typed receivers etc.; jedis 31,609 of 34,415).\n2. WRONG-EDGE RATE (266 edges, 5 per class over 62 lang x step x scope x name classes, seeded random; each judged against the source by a reviewer reading caller + target; 182 CORRECT / 84 WRONG / 9 UNDECIDABLE; WRONG verdicts spot-checked in source). Size-weighted estimate: ~5,700 of 23,819 shared-resolver edges WRONG (24%), ~8.6% of all resolved call edges. Per class sample is n=5: the per-class rates below are coarse.\n   By step: exact 20,585 edges ~19% wrong; ambiguous 2,151 ~80%; path_hint 725 ~0% (8/8 correct); suffix 182 ~15%; suffix_ambiguous 117 ~68%; soft_hint_fallback 59 100% (10/10 wrong).\n   By step x scope: a NON-UNIQUE pick (ambiguous / suffix_ambiguous) or soft-hint fallback WITHOUT in-file/import evidence: 45/57 judged wrong (~80%); WITH it: 0/13 wrong. Exact + scoped + unique name: 1/21 wrong; exact + no scope + shared name: 10/20 wrong.\n   By language (pooled class rates, coarse): rust ~49% of its 5,388; java ~38%; cpp ~25%; js ~22%; go ~20%; ts ~17%; c ~6%; python ~5%.\n3. MECHANISM of the 84 WRONG (hand-classified): MEMBER/QUALIFIED CALL BOUND BY ITS BARE MEMBER NAME, receiver or qualifier ignored ~55 (path.join -> Builder.join / a #[test] fn join; status.code() -> Language::code; fs::create_dir -> Test::create_dir; subject.subscribe -> in-repo subscribe), mostly to std/external methods -- which also HIDES the I/O crossing from io-boundaries/taint (std::fs::create_dir read as a project method). KIND MISMATCH ~9 (C call bound to a same-named STRUCT while the static function is in the same file; macro arguments; Rust struct-variant literals; a shell command bound to a file). External/global same-name function without import ~6 (libc unsetenv, mocha beforeEach). Wrong pick among in-repo duplicates ~7 (wire.go vs wire_gen.go; .d.ts vs .js). Right target, wrong edge type (function passed as a value) 4 -- not this item. LEXICAL LOCALS / PARAMETERS: 2 (+3 local object-literal methods).\n\nDECISIONS (pre-approved; reasoning recorded here):\n- The wrong-edge rate is NOT small: phase 2 proceeds.\n- PHASE 2 = the one-site rule the data supports: in the shared resolvers, a pick among 2+ candidates (ambiguous / suffix_ambiguous) and a soft-hint fallback is returned UNRESOLVED (symbol None, candidates kept) unless the pick is defined in the caller's own file. This is WI-malur's ambiguity guard (3+ -> unresolved) extended to 2 when nothing in scope backs the pick, through the unresolved path analyzers already handle. Unresolved, not a lower resolution_score: at ~80% wrong such an edge is more likely wrong than right, and a graded edge still reads as an in-repo call to every consumer (slice, dead code, taint reach); ADR-0039 ruling 6 governs how a resolved edge is graded, not whether a guess is emitted. Same-directory is NOT evidence (non-exact same_dir picks ~29-80% wrong). PRE-REGISTERED EFFECT: ~2,290 corpus edges become unresolved (rust ~2,240), of which ~80% (~1,830) are wrong and ~20% (~460) are correct edges LOST; path_hint and exact hits unchanged; no edge GAINED. Refuted if the A/B moves edges outside those steps, or a read sample of the lost edges is mostly correct.\n- PHASE 3 RE-SCOPED by the evidence: the dominant residual is CALL SHAPE, not lexical scope (locals 2/84). A core lexical binder generalising _ScopeBinder/ScopeStack has little measured payoff on this corpus and is NOT built now. Instead: a call-shape/kind admit predicate in the shared resolvers (a member call does not bind a free function; a call does not bind a struct/variable/file), adopted analyzer by analyzer, largest measured share first (rust, then java/js). The lexical binder stays open as a residual for the languages whose members ARE lexical (ocaml INV-hakof, python INV-hovoj, js WI-dagig), which this corpus cannot measure."  # a630
- op: discuss  # 0c06
  at: '2026-11-02T04:41:48Z'  # 0c06
  by: agent  # 0c06
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0c06
  clock: 3  # 0c06
  nonce: 0c06  # 0c06
  message: "PHASE 2 SHIPPED: PR #2004 (merged; HEAD c711696). Shared resolvers no longer pick among 2+ same-named candidates without evidence: a tie is unresolved, candidates kept, unless exactly one candidate is in the caller's own file (match_type caller_file). Plus: ListNameResolver.too_ambiguous() for the dart/groovy/csharp/kotlin 3+ pre-checks (they `continue`d on \"not found with candidates\" and would have DROPPED calls on a 2-way tie); ListNameResolver(variants=True) for Go's package scope (same-named entries are build-constraint variants of one function: still bind); lookup(admit=...) call-shape filter, first adopter Go (package-qualified pkg.F() admits no methods: log.Err() -> func Err, not (*Handler).Err; keyed on go.py's package_qualified, NOT import_path_hint, which a.NewFoo().Bar() also sets); core defer_unresolved_tie() wired at the 14 analyzer sites that defer weak cross-class picks to the inherited_calls Site-1 walker (INV-fahub), so a tie among methods keeps its enclosing_class.\n\nPRE-REGISTRATION vs RESULT (A/B dev 64a87d13ff vs this tree, same input, per call site with call_lines expanded; lab jomik_phase1_11012026/ab/):\n- Lost resolved call sites: predicted ~2,290 -> 2,404 (just 2,327; knex 51; jedis 21; tmux 5). By before-step: ambiguous 2,123, suffix_ambiguous 109, call_lines of those 170, other 2 (knex inherited-method).\n- Lost edges' judged sample: predicted ~80% wrong -> 26 WRONG / 8 CORRECT (76%). So ~1,830 wrong edges removed and ~570 correct edges lost (recall-loss direction, as pre-registered).\n- Gained: predicted 0 -> 100. NOT a refutation of the claim but a deviation, explained: knex 99 are inherited-calls-linker recoveries from DEFERRED ties (read 8: 5 correct, 2 wrong, 1 undecidable); jedis 1 method-call-recovery. Python, Go, C, C++, TS, express (and self-analysis): 0 lost / 0 gained.\n- REFUTED SUB-RULE, WITHDRAWN BEFORE MERGE: soft_hint_fallback -> unresolved. Corpus judged 10/10 wrong, but on the recorded aardvark-dns two-arm fixture rust-analyzer (SCIP) CONFIRMS 20/20 of the edges it removed (backend.lookup() on a parameter typed DNSBackend). The difference is the receiver's type, which the resolver cannot see; soft_hint kept as it was, its docstring records why. With it kept, aardvark-dns's call edges are identical before/after (SCIP oracle).\n- INSTRUMENT LESSONS: (1) a diff by (src,dst,line) without expanding meta.call_lines reports a dedup representative moving as lost+gained (fixed); (2) a self-analysis A/B whose INPUT is the edited tree measures the edit, not the change (re-run on the base tree: 0/0).\n\nNOT ADDRESSED (residual, measured): the EXACT step (unique short name) is ~19% wrong (~3,800 est. edges), dominated by member/qualified calls bound by the bare member name (Rust tmp.path().join(\"x\") -> just's builtin `fn join`; std methods bound to unique in-repo methods). Next increment = phase 3 re-scoped (call-shape admit predicate adopted per analyzer, Rust first: a method call cannot bind a free function).\nNoted, not acted on: java.py and ruby.py construct a method_resolver and never call it (dead parameter)."  # 0c06
