  clock: 10  # e431
  nonce: e431  # e431
  message: "LAST-WRITE-WINS REGISTRY FAMILY: MEASURED, RE-SCOPED (2026-11-02, dev 116e1e3039; lab ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lwr_11022026/RESULT.md, collisions.py). The five members share a SHAPE (a non-unique name key, one entry) but not a site: WI-dabul = core TreeSitterAnalyzer.register_symbol (global_symbols[symbol.name] = symbol), WI-povub = js_ts Case 2, WI-hahud = py symbol_by_name for route views, WI-hubik = swift return-type registry keyed without labels, WI-sipoj = tests passing by tmpfs order. Exposure measured from surveys of 11 repos (go, js, ts, python, c, cpp, rust, java): a call from a file that defines its own same-named callable, bound to another file (wrong by construction) = 6 edges, 5 read wrong (tmux 3x static cmp_procs across osdep-*bsd.c, tmux names.c, jedis RedisVersion = WI-jajaf); binds to a collided name from a third file: jotai 380, just 101, wire 99, knex 47, git-crypt 29 -- read samples correct (jotai 12/12, knex 6/6) or PLATFORM VARIANTS (git-crypt coprocess-unix/win32, tmux osdep-<os>.c: one of N equivalent files), just's 2 wrong are call shape. So ~5 wrong of ~66k resolved: a core list-valued registry is NOT built (rule 10, measured payoff small; the corpus cannot see llvm, python route collisions or swift overloads). Members fixed on their own repros instead: WI-povub + WI-sipoj DONE in PR #2021 (relative namespace/require member binds in the named file; knex 2 wrong -> correct, 0 lost). Remaining: WI-dabul (P3 llvm declare vs define), WI-hubik (P3 swift overloads), WI-hahud (P4). Not filed, seen: C/C++ platform-variant files are bound to an arbitrary one of N (the Go variants=True shape), and tmux's static cmp_procs shadowing (3 edges)."  # e431
- op: discuss  # b286
  at: '2026-11-03T02:15:12Z'  # b286
  by: agent  # b286
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b286
  clock: 11  # b286
  nonce: b286  # b286
  message: "CLOSING RATIONALE (2026-11-02, owner agreed in session: \"what we have already done on jomik is fine, but whatever is left of it was misconceived\"). WHAT WAS MISCONCEIVED: the acceptance, not the plan. The item was a measurement-first hypothesis (chokepoint fix instead of 29 one-by-one fixes; re-scope if phase 1 refutes) and phase 1 did its job: the lexical half was refuted (locals 2 of 84), the call-shape half held, and the one-site tie rule shipped. The flaw was writing a 29-member list, grouped by a clustering sweep on resemblance, into the acceptance as \"each member closed on its own repro\" without tracing whether any member passes through the shared resolvers. The 11-02 re-check answered that: 26 of 27 do not. The acceptance was unreachable by the mechanism from the day of filing. Second nuance: the call-shape adopters were never one-site either; Go, Rust, JS/TS and Java were each an analyzer change with its own A/B. They worked, and they refute the \"instead of one by one\" framing while succeeding.\nWHAT THE 25 RELEASED MEMBERS SHARE (read item by item today; descriptions, not family labels): every one is a lookup that discards information it had at the call site and then consults a bare-name table holding one entry. Four mechanisms: (A) POSITION, a binding in scope at the call site ignored for a file- or repo-wide table: INV-hakof, WI-dagig, WI-lijab, WI-lijin, WI-rafin, INV-hovoj, WI-fogoj, WI-kizih, WI-masov, WI-nazub; (B) the IMPORT/ALIAS BINDING of the root dropped: WI-vipir, INV-damas, WI-tular, WI-kazut, WI-zafin; (C) KIND/SHAPE/LANGUAGE ADMIT missing: WI-dibiv, WI-surid, WI-zutof, WI-vuzon, WI-zurum; (D) a KEY too coarse in a single-entry registry: WI-dabul, WI-hahud, WI-hubik, WI-niriz; WI-bohih is external-ref emission, not a wrong bind. Only ~5 of the 25 go through the shared resolvers; the rest hit private tables, which is why a resolver-level change cannot reach them. A core positional binder would not close group A either: the language-independent rule is small, the scope WALK is per language, and in the three languages that already have a binder (js_ts _ScopeBinder 213 lines, py _pyscope 273, go _GoLocalScope 92) 6 of the 10 A-items are open because specific lookup SITES bypass it. A one-site cross-language gate (demote a resolved call when the caller's own file or enclosing type declares a callable of that name) cannot see bindings that mint no symbol and measured 6 edges across 11 repos.\nWHAT BATCHES, BY LANGUAGE NOT BY ITEM (mechanism groupings; volumes mostly unmeasured, the items say so): js_ts, route Case 2 / new Foo() / middleware args through _ScopeBinder (WI-dagig, WI-lijin; dagig has a live io-boundaries repro); python, carry function-local imports in ScopeStack frames, the fold _pyscope anticipates (WI-kizih, WI-masov, likely INV-hovoj); go, key var_types by _GoLocalScope (WI-nazub); go value-receiver admit, the #2006 Rust rule ported (WI-surid: 306 function + 11 struct + 158 field mis-binds measured on cert-manager, the largest number in the set). Trivial wirings of maps already built and dropped: INV-damas, WI-tular. Kind filters on a private table: WI-dibiv, WI-zutof. Linker adoptions of hooks that exist: WI-vuzon (languages=), WI-zafin, WI-vipir. Registry key designs with nothing in common: group D. Needing a scope model that does not exist: INV-hakof, WI-rafin, WI-lijab, WI-fogoj."  # b286
- op: update  # 9427
  at: '2026-11-03T02:15:12Z'  # 9427
  by: agent  # 9427
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9427
  clock: 12  # 9427
  nonce: '9427'  # 9427
  set:  # 9427
    status: done  # 9427
    title: "Core name resolution, as shipped: the shared resolvers return a same-name tie unresolved without caller-file evidence; call-shape admits adopted in Go, Rust, JS/TS and Java (the core lexical binder and a list-valued registry were measured small and not built)"  # 9427
    description: "PRE-APPROVED BY THE OWNER (2026-11-01), END TO END. Every phase below, every design choice inside it, every PR (auto-pr, self-merge), any ADR edit or new ADR, and any re-scoping the evidence calls for are approved in advance. The owner's expectation: when the agent or a subagent is uncertain, it INVESTIGATES CAREFULLY — in light of docs/adr/, docs/hypergumbo-spec.md and the hypergumbo spirit (positive claims over inferred emptiness, a wrong edge is worse than a missing one, measure before sizing, ship the scope you measured, structural over per-instance) — and DECIDES, recording the reasoning on this item. Do not interrupt work to ask the owner a question the owner would almost certainly answer with \"investigate carefully and come back with a recommended path forward\": do that investigation yourself and proceed on your recommendation. Escalate only what investigation genuinely cannot settle (e.g. two owner rulings that collide), and then bring a recommendation, not an open question.\n\nCLOSED 2026-11-02, RE-SCOPED ON THE OWNER'S AGREEMENT THE SAME DAY. The title and the sections below state what was measured and shipped. The original filing (the three-phase plan, the 29-member family list, the \"each member closed on its own repro\" acceptance) is in the ops history; the discussion entries dated 2026-11-02 hold the evidence and the lab paths.\n\nPREMISE, AS FILED AND AS MEASURED. Filed: the shared resolvers (NameResolver / ListNameResolver, imported by 77 of 143 language modules) are a chokepoint through which ~29 per-analyzer short-name defects could be fixed once, by giving them a lexical scope and a call-shape filter. Measured (phase 1: 11 repos, 66,414 resolved call edges, 266 judged against source): 36% of resolved call edges come from a shared-resolver lookup; ~24% of those are wrong (~8.6% of all resolved call edges); the wrong edges are member or qualified calls bound by their bare member name (~55 of 84), kind mismatches (~9) and external same-name functions (~6); lexical locals and parameters were 2 of 84. The call-shape half of the premise held; the lexical half was refuted, and the core binder was not built.\n\nSHIPPED (each with a per-call-site corpus A/B, pre-registered where it tightened an activation gate):\n1. PR #2004, shared resolvers: a pick among 2+ same-named candidates (ambiguous / suffix_ambiguous) is UNRESOLVED, candidates kept, unless exactly one is in the caller's own file; ListNameResolver.too_ambiguous(); variants=True for Go build-constraint twins; lookup(admit=...) call-shape hook, first adopter Go (pkg.F() admits no method); defer_unresolved_tie() at the 14 INV-fahub sites. 2,404 call sites unresolved (just 2,327), judged 76% wrong; 100 gained (knex inherited-calls recoveries). soft_hint_fallback -> unresolved was pre-registered, refuted by the aardvark-dns rust-analyzer oracle (20/20 correct), withdrawn before merge.\n2. PR #2006, Rust: a method call admits no free function. just: 241 wrong binds removed, ~2,011 correct builder-chain binds gained; SCIP oracle unchanged.\n3. PR #2017, JS/TS: a callee through a binding of a module the repo positively shows external (Node core, or a registry dependency the importing package.json declares) is that module's, never in-repo; unresolved with the module as hint. express / knex / jotai lost 135 / 2,453 / 7 sites, gained 0, 59 of 59 read wrong. knex io-boundaries afterwards: 1,515 chains gained, 0 lost, subprocess 3 -> 6.\n4. PR #2019, Java: inherited_calls Site-2 step 3 (method nowhere on the receiver type's in-repo chain) emits `uses` to the type, not `calls`, so it is no longer a method_call_recovery class hint. jedis 958 sites unresolved (10 of 10 sampled wrong), commons-compress 184; 2 gained, wrong. Java joining the strict same-name guard was pre-registered and REFUTED (of the edges it would drop, commons-compress 457 correct / 42 wrong) -> WI-jajaf.\n5. PR #2021, JS/TS: a member of a relatively imported module binds in the file the import names (WI-povub, WI-sipoj done).\n\nMEASURED AND NOT BUILT. (a) The core lexical binder: locals and parameters were 2 of 84 wrong edges. (b) A core list-valued symbol registry for the last-write-wins family: ~5 wrong of ~66k resolved call edges on the corpus (lab lwr_11022026/RESULT.md).\n\nRELEASED FROM THIS ITEM (owner, 2026-11-02). The 29 members a clustering sweep attached by resemblance were never traced to the shared resolvers; the 11-02 member re-check found 26 of 27 do not pass through the step #2004 changed, so the filed acceptance was not reachable by this item's mechanism. They are ordinary analyzer items again, at their own priority, with no link to this one: INV-hakof, WI-dagig, WI-lijab, WI-lijin, WI-rafin, WI-vipir, WI-vuzon, WI-zurum, INV-hovoj, WI-fogoj, WI-kizih, WI-masov, WI-nazub, INV-damas, WI-bohih, WI-tular, WI-dibiv, WI-surid, WI-zutof, WI-kazut, WI-niriz, WI-zafin, WI-dabul, WI-hahud, WI-hubik. Done under this item: WI-povub, WI-sipoj. Folded earlier: WI-jalal (into WI-surid), WI-pagoj (into WI-dabul). What they share and how they group is in the closing discussion entry.\n\nRESIDUALS FILED FROM THIS WORK: WI-jajaf (java.py stamps an import-resolved receiver_type_id, then Java leaves _LEGACY_SITE2_LANGS); a measured note on WI-sudum (method_call_recovery never checks that a class hint is the receiver).\n\nACCEPTANCE, AS CLOSED: phase-1 numbers recorded here; a per-call-site corpus A/B for every PR, lost and gained, with the correct edges lost counted; no shrink-only baseline raised; awaits_bakeoff_validation KEPT until a cohort runs (11-02 drain: #2004 inconclusive, #2006 moved)."  # 9427
